<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Interpret cultural differences in communication. Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret cultural differences by using simple examples and gestures (e.g., says, "In Panama, people use palm leaves to make roofs." and gestures to a roof or points out a window). Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions by pointing to visuals (e.g., pointing to the map and saying, "The park is here, not there."). Can resolve misunderstandings about locations using simple phrases (e.g., saying, "This is the library." and pointing it on a map).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of buildings from different parts of Panama, showcasing both traditional (e.g., bohío with palm leaf roof) and modern (e.g., skyscraper with glass/steel) materials. Students are asked to identify materials and share where they might see such buildings. (e.g., "Where can you see a roof like this?", "What is this building made of?"). This activates prior knowledge of materials and locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to interpret and explain differences in building materials using simple language and gestures. For example, pointing to a picture of a palm leaf roof: "In rural areas, people often use <b>palm leaves</b> for roofs. It's a <b>tradition</b>. (gestures to roof). But in the city, there are many <b>modern buildings</b> with <b>zinc</b> or <b>concrete</b> roofs." The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /s/ in "street," "spacious."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do people in the city usually have palm leaf roofs?" (No, they don't.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a house made of palm leaves 'traditional' or 'modern'?" (Traditional.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The park is *across from* the school,' and point to the wrong place, how can you help me understand?" (You can say, "No, the park is *here*," and point to the correct place.)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple diagram or a stylized map of a Panamanian town square, showing key landmarks (e.g., church, town hall, a traditional house, a modern bank) and roads. It includes labels for "traditional building," "modern building," "fountain," "statue," "park," "library," "market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will interpret simple information about locations and building types on a visual, identifying traditional vs. modern elements within a town square setting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher presents the map and reads short descriptions.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are some of the places you see in the town square?" "Are there only traditional buildings?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher makes statements based on the map. E.g., "There is a <b>market</b> next to the <b>town hall</b>." (Students indicate True/False). "The <b>fountain</b> is in front of the <b>statue</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match descriptions to images on the map (e.g., "A building with a metal roof for selling food" to 'market').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using imperatives, sequencing words, "there is/there are," and prepositions of place to describe locations and procedures, while focusing on clarifying information and interpreting differences. They also practice pronunciation of words like "street," "monument," "tradition."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Map Description Cloze:</b> Students receive a simple map (similar to Stage 2) and sentences with blanks. They complete the sentences using "there is/are" and prepositions of place. E.g., "_______ a park _______ the library." "_______ a traditional house _______ the modern bank." This reinforces accurate grammar for location descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Explaining Differences:</b> In pairs, students receive two contrasting images (e.g., a traditional thatched roof and a modern tiled roof, or a historic monument and a new pedestrian bridge). Student A explains one image, and Student B asks questions to clarify. Then they switch. E.g., Student A: "This is a <b>traditional roof</b>. People in my grandfather's town use <b>palm leaves</b>." Student B: "Are all roofs in Panama made of palm leaves?" Student A clarifies: "No, in the city, there are many <b>buildings</b> with <b>modern</b> roofs." The teacher monitors for appropriate use of vocabulary and clarifying phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Clarify the Route":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has an identical map of the town square with a starting point and a destination marked. One student gives directions using imperatives and sequencing words (e.g., "<b>First, walk straight</b>. <b>Then, turn left</b> at the <b>intersection</b>."). The other student follows on their map. If there's a misunderstanding, the follower must ask for clarification (e.g., "Do you mean *here*?" or "Is the library <b>across from</b> the market or <b>next to</b> it?"). The giver must clarify using gestures or simple rephrasing (e.g., "No, the <b>library</b> is <b>here</b>," pointing on the map, "It's <b>next to</b> the <b>market</b>."). This directly practices clarifying misunderstandings about locations.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group project presentation focusing on mediation. Students will finalize and present their "Community Landmark Collage" to another group. They must explain their choices, clarify locations, and interpret traditional vs. modern elements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form their original project groups. They retrieve their "Community Landmark Collage" (started in previous lessons).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is paired with another group. Group A presents their collage to Group B.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">During the presentation, Group A members collectively explain their collage:
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They use imperatives and sequencing words to describe how they created it (e.g., "<b>First, we drew</b> the <b>park</b>. <b>Then, we added</b> the <b>library</b> <b>next to</b> it.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They describe the locations of landmarks using "there is/are" and prepositions (e.g., "<b>There is</b> a <b>statue</b> in the center of the <b>town square</b>.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Crucially, they interpret cultural differences in building materials or traditions, using simple examples and gestures (e.g., "This building has a <b>traditional roof</b> made of <b>tiles</b>. In other parts of Panama, people use more <b>modern materials</b> like <b>zinc</b> for roofs," gesturing to different parts of the collage).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group B actively listens and asks clarifying WH-questions (e.g., "<b>Where is</b> the <b>market</b>?", "What is a '<b>bohío</b>' roof?"). Group A must clarify any misunderstandings about locations or cultural elements by pointing to visuals and using simple phrases (e.g., "No, the <b>park is here</b>, not <b>there</b>," or "A '<b>bohío</b>' is a <b>traditional</b> house, made with <b>natural materials</b> like <b>palm leaves</b>," making a roofing gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then switch roles.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and peer assessment during the group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Community Landmark Collage" and mediates a discussion about it. They explain the content and clarify any questions from another group or the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist/Peer feedback based on):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of /s/ clusters and syllable stress in multi-syllable words (e.g., "traditional," "monument").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, prepositions, adverbs).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation when describing traditional vs. modern building materials (interprets cultural differences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effectiveness in clarifying misunderstandings about locations on the collage using simple phrases and pointing (e.g., "The park is here, not there.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group and with the listening group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "there is/there are" and imperatives/sequencing words for arrangement and location.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult about explaining your collage to another group?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help your classmates understand when they were confused about a location or a traditional material?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting traditional or modern building material you learned about in this lesson?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use phrases like 'The park is here, not there' or gestures to help someone understand directions in English?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The focus on mediation (interpreting cultural differences and clarifying misunderstandings) was effectively integrated through the collage project. Students were engaged in explaining their choices and actively asking/answering clarifying questions. The explicit modeling of gestures and simple explanations for cultural differences was crucial. Some groups may need more scaffolding in forming clarifying questions. Next time, I could provide a sentence starter for "clarifying misunderstanding" questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Look at the Traditional and Modern Building Materials" and the unit on "The Town Square." The next lesson will transition to a new theme, possibly exploring community services or local events, building on students' ability to describe locations and communicate clearly in English.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>